ZTEXT0010000247f1de1cccf0000000
Ezero implementation contract (original Zero implementation)

Zero is the system language; Ezero is the high-level language built on it.
The frontend, packer, runtime, native compiler command and build/check tools
are original Zero. Targets are Linux x86-64, Windows x64 and Zero OS native
32/64-bit applications. Guest applications use the owned memory-stream ABI
and share kernel memory; application loading is not process isolation.

Source grammar
UTF-8 source; validate UTF-8 and reject embedded NUL. Text needs no quotes.
Bare unknown words become text; an already-bound bare name reads its value.
Adjacent values form a typed phrase: 3 банана retains number3 and textбанана.
Consecutive literal text words form one text segment (space-normalized).
Phrases print their parts separated by spaces and support INDEX/SIZE/each.
If all phrase parts are text, MAKE_PHRASE may merge them into one text value.
Optional quoted text remains an escape hatch for punctuation/reserved names,
with \n \r \t \\ \" escapes; examples must use the unquoted form.
The reserved word empty produces empty text; true/false remain numeric1/0.
Newline terminates an ordinary statement; blank lines and # comments allowed.
Names: ASCII letter/underscore then letters/digits/underscore, 1..31 bytes.
No @, declared types or trailing punctuation required.
expression => name                 create or replace a variable
expression => show                 print its value and a newline
expression => return               return from a function
expression => repeat [ statements ]
expression => when [ statements ] ~ [ statements ]
expression => each name [ statements ]
name :: parameter | parameter [ statements ]  top-level function definition
Definitions may have zero parameters. Register a function before parsing its
body, allowing self-recursion. Functions must be defined before use; no nested
function definitions. Implicit function return is numeric zero. Top-level
return is rejected. Parameters and function assignments are local. Reads first
resolve known locals, then earlier global bindings. No closures/imports yet.
Statements inside blocks still end at a newline or the closing ]. A newline
after ] may precede ~. Arrays/calls/grouped expressions may span lines.

Expressions: decimal u64 literals, unquoted UTF-8 text/phrases, variables, [a | b | c],
function(arg | arg), size(value), list[index], grouping, + - *, comparisons
== != < <= > >=. Normal precedence: postfix, multiplication, add/sub, one
comparison. No unary minus/division; checked unsigned arithmetic.
true and false are numeric1/0. Conditions require a number,0 means false.
+ handles number+number (checked), text+text, text+number, number+text,
and list+list. Other combinations fail clearly. Lists can contain mixed and
nested values. User indices are ONE-BASED: first element1, last element=size.
Index0 and index>size are runtime bounds errors; list/phrase indexing only.
size accepts lists/phrases (element count) and text (Unicode scalar count). Equality compares numbers and text by content;
list equality is rejected (do not silently compare identity).
show prints numbers in decimal, raw text, and lists as [a | b | c].
Ordinary numeric tokens are number values, including inside an unquoted
phrase; quoted text is optional, never required for the common examples.
For example 3 банана => purchase; purchase[1]+2 => show prints5;
purchase[2] => show printsбанана. User-facing documentation calls this Ezero,
the existing system language remains Zero.
No manual addresses/allocations are exposed in Ezero. Heap objects immutable;
runtime owns reclamation and bounds checks.

Limits
source65536 bytes;4096-byte decoded string;16384 total constant bytes;
1536 instructions;64 functions including main;16 parameters;256 variables per
scope including hidden loop slots;32 expression/block nesting levels;
2048 evaluation values;64 active function calls; heap262144 bytes per space.
Capacity, uninitialized variable, type and arithmetic errors must be explicit,
never out-of-bounds writes or silent wrapping.

Frontend output is a binary instruction/data package, completely buffered
before output. Compile errors: nonzero exit, human-readable ASCII diagnostic
with source line on stdout. The native command publishes no failed output.
Successful package has the following little-endian u64 header (64 bytes):
0 magic0x31564d4f52455a45;8 totalBytes;16 instructionCount;24 functionCount;
32 globalCount;40 constantByteCount;48 version1;56 reserved0.
Then functionCount records of32 bytes: entryInstruction,parameterCount,
localCount,reserved0. Main index0: entry0,params0,locals0 (uses globalCount).
Then instructionCount records of32 bytes: opcode,a,b,sourceLine.
Then constantByteCount raw bytes. PUSH_TEXT offsets are relative to constants.
Jump/function entries use instruction indices, not byte addresses.
No separate external runtime library: generated .zero contains owned data
initialization and the original Zero runtime, compiled as one executable.
This first version uses our instruction interpreter inside that executable;
it is not direct native code emission for every Ezero expression.

Opcodes
0 HALT
1 PUSH_NUMBER a
2 PUSH_TEXT a=constantOffset,b=length
3 LOAD_GLOBAL a=index
4 STORE_GLOBAL a=index (pops)
5 LOAD_LOCAL a=index
6 STORE_LOCAL a=index (pops)
7 ADD
8 SUBTRACT
9 MULTIPLY
10 EQUAL
11 NOT_EQUAL
12 LESS
13 LESS_EQUAL
14 GREATER
15 GREATER_EQUAL
16 MAKE_LIST a=count (pop ordered values, push list)
17 INDEX (pop index then list; push selected value)
18 SIZE
19 SHOW (pop value, newline)
20 DROP
21 JUMP a=instructionIndex
22 JUMP_FALSE a=instructionIndex (pops numeric condition)
23 CALL a=functionIndex,b=argumentCount
24 RETURN (pops result; preserve caller evaluation values)
25 DUPLICATE
26 MAKE_PHRASE a=count (typed ordered parts, space-separated rendering)

Frontend lowers repeat into a hidden remaining-count slot and >0 loop;
each into hidden list/index slots and SIZE/INDEX loop, starting index1 and
continuing while index<=size. Internal bytecode indices remain unchanged. Expressions evaluated
once on entry to these constructs. User bindings can first occur in a block;
read before a runtime assignment is an explicit uninitialized-variable error.
Hidden slots cannot collide with user names. Temporaries are frame-local.

Runtime prefix initializes exactly these shared integration variables:
:reserve[2097152] => @ez_mem.
@ez_mem => @ez_program.
0 => @ez_program_bytes.
0 => @ez_encoded. 0 => @ez_decode_i. 0 => @ez_decode_n.
0 => @ez_decode_a. 0 => @ez_decode_b.
Packer emits initialization using ONLY these eight declared names, reusing
decode temporaries; sets ez_program_bytes; no additional declarations.
Prefix + packer output + runtime.zero.part must compile under64-variable cap.
Packer input limit70000 bytes; each generated string <=2048 hex characters.
Program initialization goes only into ez_mem[0..262143]. Runtime state lives
above that region, within the same2MiB reservation. No further reserve calls.
Runtime allocation, copy-GC roots, frames and stack layout belong to runtime
agent and must be documented in MEMORY.ztext. On runtime error print an ASCII
Ezero error with source line and exit65; output before failure may exist.

Canonical0.10 host interoperability extension
27 READ_FILE;28 WRITE_FILE;29 FILE_EXISTS;30 ARGUMENTS;31 HOST.
All five require a=b=0. Frontend builtins readfile(path),writefile(path|text),
fileexists(path),arguments(),host(). Strict bounded UTF-8 external text;
writefile exclusively publishes a new file and never replaces a destination.
See HOST.ztext for the complete host interoperability contract.

Text convenience extension
32 LINES;33 JOIN. Both require a=b=0. LINES consumes one text value and pushes
a list. JOIN consumes a list then text separator and pushes text. Arity is
checked by the frontend; runtime checks types and complete allocation bounds.
Token314 lines maps to builtin70/opcode32; token315 join maps to71/33.
Both operations are independent of host APIs and run on guest32/64 targets.
The language guide defines newline, empty-input and strict text-item behavior.

File service extension
34 CREATE; 35 DELETE; 36 WATCH; 37 SEARCH; 38 SAVE; 39 GO; 40 DISKS.
All require a=b=0. CREATE/DELETE/GO consume one text path and push number 1
on success. SAVE consumes path and text, pushes the byte count. WATCH/SEARCH
consume a path and push a one-based text list; DISKS has no operand and pushes
a text list. Paths and staged results undergo strict UTF-8 validation before
managed values are created. The entire list allocation is reserved before GC.
Native adapters and the guest kernel service expose the same primitive calling
shape; availability, path/content limits and durability remain platform-specific.
READ_FILE/WRITE_FILE/FILE_EXISTS now also use the guest whole-file service.
ARGUMENTS remains native-only. See HOST.ztext for the user-visible contract.

Normalized frontend tokens 316..322 map to builtins 72..78 and opcodes 34..40.
read aliases readfile (token 309/opcode 27); rm aliases del (317/35). Aliases do
not add opcodes. The scanner keeps an initial letter-colon-bar sequence inside
a path word, so A:|file and B:| do not split at the root separator. All other
bars retain their existing list/argument separator meaning.
